# 7. Analyze Heap Dump

Popular tools:

* Eclipse MAT
* VisualVM
* JProfiler
* YourKit

For example, with Eclipse MAT you might see:

```text
Class                         Shallow Heap    Retained Heap

byte[]                         1 MB           1.2 GB
ArrayList                       24 B           1.2 GB
HashMap                         48 B           500 MB
String                          24 B           300 MB
```

The important concept is:

## Shallow Heap

Memory occupied by the object itself.

Example:

```java
class User {

    String name;
    Address address;
}
```

The `User` object itself might consume only a few dozen bytes.

---

# 8. Retained Heap

Retained heap is much more important for leak investigation.

Imagine:

```text
CACHE
 |
 +---- User
 |      |
 |      +---- Address
 |      +---- String
 |
 +---- User
        |
        +---- Address
```

If removing `CACHE` would make all those objects eligible for GC, then `CACHE` has a large retained heap.

So when investigating memory leaks:

> **Look at retained heap, not just shallow heap.**

---

# 9. Common Heap Problems

## Problem 1 — Static collection

Bad:

```java
private static final List<User> USERS =
        new ArrayList<>();
```

If you continuously add:

```java
USERS.add(user);
```

memory grows indefinitely.

### Solution

Use bounded cache:

```java
Cache<String, User> cache;
```

or:

```java
Caffeine
Redis
Ehcache
```

with:

```text
maximumSize
TTL
expiration
```

---

# 10. Problem 2 — Unbounded cache

Bad:

```java
Map<String, User> cache = new HashMap<>();

public void add(User user) {

    cache.put(user.getId(), user);
}
```

If millions of users are inserted:

```text
HashMap
   |
   +---- millions of objects
```

### Solution

Bound it:

```text
maximum entries
TTL
LRU
expiration
```

---

# 11. Problem 3 — Too many objects

Consider:

```java
List<User> users = repository.findAll();
```

Suppose database contains:

```text
10 million rows
```

You just loaded all 10 million into memory.

Bad.

Instead:

```java
Page<User> users =
        repository.findAll(PageRequest.of(0, 100));
```

or process using streaming/batching where appropriate.

---

# 12. Problem 4 — Huge JSON response

Bad:

```java
@GetMapping("/users")
public List<User> users() {

    return userRepository.findAll();
}
```

Suppose there are:

```text
5 million users
```

Your application may create:

```text
Database objects
        ↓
Java objects
        ↓
JSON objects
        ↓
JSON String / buffers
        ↓
HTTP response
```

Memory can explode.

### Solution

Use pagination:

```http
GET /users?page=0&size=100
```

---

# 13. Problem 5 — ThreadLocal leak

Example:

```java
private static final ThreadLocal<byte[]> DATA =
        new ThreadLocal<>();

public void process() {

    DATA.set(new byte[10 * 1024 * 1024]);

    // processing
}
```

If this executes inside a thread pool and the ThreadLocal isn't removed, data can remain associated with the long-lived worker thread.

Better:

```java
try {

    DATA.set(new byte[10 * 1024 * 1024]);

    // processing

} finally {

    DATA.remove();
}
```

---

# 14. Heap dump doesn't tell you everything

Heap dump is excellent for:

```text
Memory leak
Unexpected object growth
Large collections
Huge caches
Duplicate objects
Retained objects
```

But it does NOT directly answer:

> Why is CPU 100%?

For that you need:

# Thread Dump

---

# 15. Thread Dump

A thread dump is a snapshot of JVM threads and their current state.

Example:

```text
"http-nio-8080-exec-10"

RUNNABLE

at com.example.OrderService.process(OrderService.java:50)
at com.example.OrderController.create(OrderController.java:30)
```

It tells you:

```text
Which thread?
What state?
What code?
Waiting for what?
Holding which lock?
```

---

# 16. Java Thread States

Important states:

```text
NEW
RUNNABLE
BLOCKED
WAITING
TIMED_WAITING
TERMINATED
```

---

# 17. RUNNABLE

Example:

```java
while (true) {
    calculate();
}
```

Thread might show:

```text
RUNNABLE

at com.example.CalculationService.calculate(...)
```

Potential issue:

```text
Infinite loop
CPU-heavy computation
Busy waiting
Bad algorithm
```

---

# 18. BLOCKED

This is extremely important.

Example:

```java
public synchronized void process() {

    // long operation
}
```

Suppose:

```text
Thread-1
   |
   +-- owns lock

Thread-2
   |
   +-- waiting for Thread-1
```

Thread-2 can appear:

```text
BLOCKED
```

---

# 19. Deadlock Example

```java
public class DeadlockExample {

    private final Object lock1 = new Object();
    private final Object lock2 = new Object();

    public void method1() {

        synchronized (lock1) {

            System.out.println("Lock1 acquired");

            synchronized (lock2) {

                System.out.println("Lock2 acquired");
            }
        }
    }

    public void method2() {

        synchronized (lock2) {

            System.out.println("Lock2 acquired");

            synchronized (lock1) {

                System.out.println("Lock1 acquired");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Thread A:

```text
Lock1
  ↓
waiting for Lock2
```

Thread B:

```text
Lock2
  ↓
waiting for Lock1
```

Result:

```text
Thread A ────── waits ──────> Lock2
   ↑                            |
   |                            |
   |                            ↓
Lock1 <────── waits ─────── Thread B
```

Deadlock.

---

# 20. Solution — consistent lock ordering

Always acquire locks in the same order:

```java
synchronized (lock1) {

    synchronized (lock2) {

        // work
    }
}
```

Both methods should follow:

```text
lock1
  ↓
lock2
```

Never:

```text
method1:
lock1 → lock2

method2:
lock2 → lock1
```

---

# 21. Taking Thread Dump

First:

```bash
jps -l
```

Then:

```bash
jstack 12345
```

Save it:

```bash
jstack 12345 > threaddump.txt
```

