# 25. Thread Pool Problem

Consider Spring Boot:

```java
ExecutorService executor =
        Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);
```

Then:

```java
for (int i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {

    executor.submit(() -> {

        callExternalService();

    });
}
```

Suppose external service takes:

```text
30 seconds
```

Then:

```text
10 threads
   ↓
all waiting for external service
```

New requests cannot get workers.

Application appears:

```text
VERY SLOW
```

---

# 26. Thread Pool Exhaustion

This is a common production problem.

```text
HTTP Requests
     |
     v
Tomcat Thread Pool
     |
     v
Application Thread Pool
     |
     +---- Thread 1 → DB
     +---- Thread 2 → DB
     +---- Thread 3 → DB
     +---- Thread 4 → external API
     +---- Thread 5 → external API
     +---- ...
```

If all threads are waiting:

```text
Active = maximum
Queue = growing
```

Eventually:

```text
timeouts
503
latency
request failures
```

---

# 27. Solution — Don't blindly increase threads

A common mistake:

> Application is slow → increase thread pool.

Not always correct.

If database supports only:

```text
100 connections
```

and you create:

```text
500 application threads
```

you may make things worse.

You need to understand:

```text
CPU
DB connection pool
external API
thread pool
queue
network
```

---

# 28. GC Logs

Now the third important diagnostic:

# Garbage Collection Logs

GC logs tell you:

```text
When GC occurred
Which collector
How much memory existed
How much was reclaimed
How long GC took
How frequently GC happens
```

---

# 29. Enable GC Logging — Java 17+

For Java 17:

```bash
-Xlog:gc*:file=gc.log:time,uptime,level,tags
```

Example:

```bash
java \
-Xms2g \
-Xmx2g \
-Xlog:gc*:file=/var/log/app/gc.log:time,uptime,level,tags \
-jar application.jar
```

---

# 30. Example GC Log

You might see:

```text
[2026-09-08T10:10:10.123+0530]
GC(25) Pause Young
Total heap: 2048M
Before: 1800M
After: 900M
Duration: 120ms
```

Interpretation:

```text
Before GC
1800 MB

GC

After GC
900 MB
```

So:

```text
~900 MB reclaimed
```

That's generally healthy.

---

# 31. Problematic GC Pattern

Suppose logs show:

```text
Before    After

1800 MB → 1700 MB
1900 MB → 1800 MB
1950 MB → 1900 MB
2000 MB → 1950 MB
```

GC is running but reclaiming very little.

This is suspicious.

Why?

Because most objects are still alive.

Possible reasons:

```text
Memory leak
Large cache
Long-lived references
Huge collections
Too many active requests
```

---

# 32. Young GC vs Old GC

Conceptually:

```text
Young Generation
       |
       | Minor/Young GC
       v
Survivors
       |
       v
Old Generation
       |
       | Major/Full GC
       v
Old objects
```

Most Java objects are short-lived.

Example:

```java
public Response process(Request request) {

    String temp = request.getName();

    List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();

    // temporary objects

    return response;
}
```

Many temporary objects become unreachable quickly.

Young GC can clean them.

---

# 33. Why young GC is usually cheaper

Suppose:

```text
1000 objects created
```

Only:

```text
10 objects survive
```

GC can efficiently reclaim most of them.

But suppose:

```text
1000 objects
900 survive
```

GC has more work.

If Old Gen keeps filling:

```text
Old Gen
████████████████████
```

you may eventually get:

```text
Full GC
```

or significant pauses depending on collector/configuration.

---

# 34. G1 GC

Modern Java versions commonly use:

```text
G1GC
```

G1 divides heap into regions:

```text
+---+---+---+---+---+
|   |   |   |   |   |
+---+---+---+---+---+
|   |   |   |   |   |
+---+---+---+---+---+
```

Regions can serve different roles:

```text
Eden
Survivor
Old
```

G1 attempts to meet pause-time goals while collecting regions strategically.

Example:

```bash
-XX:+UseG1GC
-XX:MaxGCPauseMillis=200
```

But:

> Don't blindly tune GC before understanding the workload.

---

# 35. Common GC Problem #1 — High Allocation Rate

Consider:

```java
public void process() {

    for (int i = 0; i < 10_000_000; i++) {

        String value =
                new String("some value");

        process(value);
    }
}
```

You're creating huge numbers of objects.

Even if objects are short-lived:

```text
Allocation rate ↑
        ↓
Young GC frequency ↑
        ↓
CPU usage ↑
```

Solution:

```text
Reduce unnecessary object creation
Reuse immutable values where appropriate
Optimize serialization
Batch operations
Avoid excessive temporary collections
```

---

# 36. Common GC Problem #2 — Memory Leak

Example:

```java
static Map<String, byte[]> cache =
        new HashMap<>();
```

Every request:

```java
cache.put(id, new byte[1024 * 1024]);
```

Then:

```text
Heap ↑
Old Gen ↑
GC frequency ↑
GC duration ↑
Eventually OOM
```

Heap dump will identify the retained objects.

---

# 37. Common GC Problem #3 — Large Object Allocation

Suppose:

```java
byte[] data = new byte[100 * 1024 * 1024];
```

That's:

```text
100 MB
```

Large allocations can put pressure on the heap and collector.

In G1, sufficiently large allocations may be treated as **humongous objects**.

Repeated large allocations can create significant GC pressure.

Solution:

```text
Streaming
Chunking
Pagination
Multipart processing
Avoid loading entire files into memory
```

