# 60. One very important interview scenario

**Interviewer:**

> Production API latency suddenly increased from 200 ms to 10 seconds. How will you troubleshoot?

A strong answer:

> First I would check application and infrastructure metrics such as CPU, memory, GC pause time, request rate, error rate, thread pool utilization, database connection pool utilization, and downstream latency.
>
> If CPU is high, I would take multiple thread dumps and identify RUNNABLE/high-CPU threads. If threads are BLOCKED, I would investigate lock contention or deadlocks. If many threads are WAITING or TIMED_WAITING, I would determine whether they are waiting on a database, external service, connection pool, queue, or other synchronization mechanism.
>
> If memory usage is high, I would inspect GC logs to determine whether the JVM is experiencing frequent GC, long pauses, or Old Generation growth. If objects continue surviving GC and heap usage keeps increasing, I would capture a heap dump and analyze retained heap, GC roots, large collections, caches, and object dominators.
>
> I would then correlate the evidence from metrics, thread dumps, GC logs, and heap dump rather than changing JVM parameters blindly.

That's a **senior-level production troubleshooting answer**.

